/*
⏱️ Time Complexity

O(rows × cols)
Each cell is visited once and then marked as water.

💾 Space Complexity

Auxiliary space: O(rows × cols) in the worst case (DFS recursion stack)

Extra structures: O(1) (in-place grid modification)
🧠 Simple intuition

Scan the grid.

When you hit land (1), DFS to consume the whole island.

Count how many cells you consume → that’s the island’s area.

Keep the maximum.

*/
class Solution {
    int maxArea = 0;
    int count = 0;
    int rows,cols;
    public int maxAreaOfIsland(int[][] grid) {
        rows = grid.length;
        cols = grid[0].length;
        for(int i=0;i<rows;i++){
            for(int j=0;j<cols;j++){
                if(grid[i][j]==1){
                    count = 0;
                    dfs(grid,i,j);
                    maxArea = Math.max(maxArea,count);
                }
            }
        }
        return maxArea;
    }
    public void dfs(int[][] grid,int r,int c){
        if(r<0 || c<0 ||
        r>=rows || c>=cols || grid[r][c]==0){
            return;
        }
        grid[r][c]=0;
        count++;
        dfs(grid,r+1,c);
        dfs(grid,r-1,c);
        dfs(grid,r,c+1);
        dfs(grid,r,c-1);
    }
}
